      "## How two bidders came to claim the same site",
      "Chandigarh offered two nursing-home sites in the same auction. Site No. 3 fetched ₹18.25 crore. Bedi Hospital’s ₹12.58 crore bid was the highest for Site No. 4. The hospital deposited the required 25%, signed an agreement to sell, then paid the remaining 75%. It asked the Administration to expedite the allotment paperwork, but no allotment letter or conveyance deed followed. The Administration considered the bid below market, cancelled it, refunded the full amount, and invited the hospital to participate in a fresh auction. It separately cancelled the agreement to sell while the hospital’s writ petition was pending. (Paras. 3–9.)",
      "The High Court allowed the Administration to proceed with an auction while withholding finalisation pending the litigation. After earlier attempts failed to produce a qualifying result, the September 2023 auction yielded a highest bid of ₹18.30 crore from the appellant. Bedi Hospital did not bid. The appellant joined the writ proceedings, but the High Court ultimately quashed the cancellation letters. The appellant and the Chandigarh Administration then appealed to the Supreme Court. (Paras. 10–12.)",
      "## Full payment did not complete the sale under these rules",
      "The Court read Rule 5 of the Chandigarh Estate Rules, 2007 alongside the auction terms and the prescribed agreement. These set out successive steps from the deposit and agreement to sell through the allotment letter and conveyance deed. For this statutory auction, the Court treated the conveyance deed as the point at which an enforceable contract came into existence. The Administration still had its reserved withdrawal power when it cancelled the bid. (Paras. 14–19.)",
      "That power was subject to public-law duties of fairness and reasonableness. The Court accepted that the difference between comparable sites auctioned on the same day gave the Administration a reason to revisit the price before the contract was final. It distinguished *Shishir Realty*, which involved the arbitrary cancellation of lease deeds that had already been granted. Here, the hospital received its money back and could participate again. (Paras. 20–22.)",
      "## Why the Court ordered a fresh auction",
      "The Supreme Court found the later auction valid and criticised the High Court for failing to address the interests that its own interim order had allowed to arise. Bedi Hospital offered to match ₹18.30 crore when asked in court. The appellant objected that it had paid the full consideration after winning a process in which the hospital chose not to participate. The Supreme Court set aside the High Court ruling and directed a fresh auction under the Chandigarh Estate Rules rather than directing allotment to either bidder. Both civil appeals were allowed, pending applications were disposed of, and the Court made no costs order. (Paras. 23–30.)",
      "The ruling turns on the stages and reserved powers in this auction’s rules. In another dispute, the relevant questions include when the governing terms create an enforceable contract and whether the authority can justify its decision under public-law standards. (Paras. 17–22.)",
