      "## How the dispute reached the Court",
      "MS Sharma Enterprises took on flooring and cladding work as NBCC’s subcontractor. Its ability to proceed depended on civil works by another contractor. The arbitrator found in 2016 that NBCC had wrongfully terminated the contract and failed to make the work fronts available. The award allowed several claims, including payment for work and extra items and repayment of an encashed guarantee, and granted interest at 10% a year on the awarded sums other than costs. It ran from September 1992 until payment. A Delhi High Court judge removed the pre-reference and pending-arbitration portions, and the Division Bench upheld that result. (Paras. 2–3.8.)",
      "## Why the wording of Clause 26 mattered",
      "Section 31(7)(a) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 lets an arbitrator award interest up to the date of the award unless the parties agreed otherwise. Sharma Enterprises argued that the final phrase “or otherwise” should take its meaning from the preceding payment categories. NBCC argued that those categories were separate and the closing language covered other situations too. The Court read the provision as listing distinct events. With no narrow common category to limit it, the residual phrase had independent work to do. (Paras. 4–14.)",
      "## Why the award was cut only in part",
      "The Court distinguished earlier cases that dealt with narrower wording or a different statutory setting. It treated the prohibition as a limit on the arbitrator’s authority, even though the dispute was longstanding and the contractor had established substantive entitlement. The Court also distinguished pre-award interest under section 31(7)(a) from post-award interest under section 31(7)(b). It dismissed the appeal against the partial setting aside. A different contract may use a differently scoped prohibition. (Paras. 10 and 15–20.)",
