    "citation": "2026 INSC 1121",
    "date": "2026-10-14",
    "title": "How the Supreme Court counted the deadline for an insolvency appeal",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** The Assistant Commissioner of Central Tax, CGST Division v. Sreenivasa Rao Ravinuthala and Another · 2026 INSC 1121 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 401",
      "**Decided** 14 October 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 7882 of 2023)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [S.V.N. Bhatti* and N.V. Anjaria, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court dismissed the tax authority’s appeal as late. It excluded the time the court registry needed to provide a certified copy, but counted the time before the authority requested it and after it received it. Those intervals totalled 76 days, beyond the 60-day maximum available under section 62 of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code. (Paras. 11–19.)",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/the-assistant-commissioner-of-central-tax-cgst-division-versus-sreenivas-0812c727)",
      "## How the deadline became decisive",
      "The National Company Law Appellate Tribunal upheld the resolution plan concerning Samyu Glass on 2 August 2023. The tax authority appealed to the Supreme Court on 21 November. The respondent raised limitation as a preliminary objection. The authority argued that the wording of section 62 tied the start of the clock to receipt of the order, and sought to distinguish that language from the wording of section 61. (Paras. 1–6.)",
      "## Excluding copy time is different from excusing delay",
      "Section 62 allows 45 days to appeal and a further period of up to 15 days that the Court may allow for sufficient cause. Section 12(2) of the Limitation Act deals with excluding time required to obtain a copy. The Court explained that a party cannot defer the start of limitation indefinitely by choosing when to apply for a certified copy. In this case it counted from pronouncement, while excluding the time actually required by the registry to produce and deliver the copy after the request. (Paras. 6–14.)",
      "## The calculation in this case",
      "The authority waited 35 days before applying on 6 September. The Court excluded the next 35 days, from the application to delivery on 11 October. The authority then waited 41 more days to file. Adding the two periods that counted gave 76 days. Only 10 days of the initial 45-day window remained when the copy was delivered. Even allowing for the maximum further 15 days, the appeal was late. The Court dismissed it on limitation, without deciding the merits of the resolution-plan challenge or making an order for costs. (Paras. 15–19.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
