    "title": "Does a nephew count as family under Uttar Pradesh’s rent law?",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Islam Hussain & Ors. v. Hajari Lal & Anr. · 2026 INSC 1123 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 412",
      "**Decided** 13 October 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 8092 of 2013)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Prashant Kumar Mishra and Shree Chandrashekhar, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court ordered eviction from one of two shops after ruling that the tenant’s nephew did not fall within the definition of family in the Uttar Pradesh Urban Buildings (Regulation of Letting, Rent and Eviction) Act, 1972. It confined relief to Shop B, where the tenant had taken his nephew into partnership. (Paras. 2–3 and 5–15.)",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/islam-hussain-ors-versus-hajari-lal-anr-aef9838c)",
      "## Two shops, one disputed partnership",
      "The landlords had let Shop A and Shop B to the first respondent. They sought eviction and recovery of arrears, alleging that the tenant had sublet Shop B by entering a partnership with his nephew. The tenant admitted the partnership. The courts below nevertheless dismissed the suit, principally because the nephew had lived with the tenant since childhood and was treated as family. (Paras. 1–4.)",
      "## Why the statutory definition controlled",
      "Section 3(g) identifies which relatives count as family for this Act. It names spouses and male lineal descendants, and covers certain parents, grandparents and unmarried, widowed, divorced or judicially separated daughters subject to the terms of the definition. The Court found that a nephew was outside that list. The respondents relied on *K.V. Muthu*, where the Court considered a foster son under a different statute and factual setting. The Court found no facts beyond the living arrangement that justified extending the Uttar Pradesh definition in this case. (Paras. 5–9.)",
      "## Why the order was limited to Shop B",
      "Once the nephew was excluded from the statutory category, the admitted partnership supplied the factual basis for the Court’s subletting conclusion concerning Shop B. The Court partly allowed the appeal and directed vacant possession of Shop B within six months. The six months were conditional on the occupants filing the usual undertaking within one week. They were also directed to clear any Shop B rent arrears within one month, continue paying rent or occupation charges until they left, and refrain from creating third-party interests while in occupation. (Paras. 12–13.) The order did not extend eviction to Shop A and made no costs order. The result turns on this Act’s definition and the facts found for the individual shop. (Paras. 10–15.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
