      "## How the case reached the Court",
      "A trial court convicted the appellant and the Meghalaya High Court affirmed. The Supreme Court reviewed the evidence relied on below, including the accounts of the informant, another witness and the child. It considered the delay in reporting as one circumstance among several and examined the accounts in light of the rest of the record. (Paras. 1–61.)",
      "## Why the conviction was set aside",
      "The Court found that the child and another witness did not identify the appellant in court, that accounts differed about his relationship to the family, and that a teacher relevant to the reporting sequence was not examined. It also found credible testimony and documentary support for the defence account that the appellant was elsewhere on the day in question. The Court considered those gaps and the reporting delay together. (Paras. 60–62.) The ruling does not turn on a general proposition that delayed reporting defeats a prosecution. The appeal was allowed, the conviction and sentence were set aside, and the appellant was to be released unless required in another case. (Paras. 62–66.)",
