    "date": "2026-10-07",
    "title": "Why personal presentation mattered in a Madras High Court election petition",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** V. Pon. Paneerselvam v. S. Regupathy and Others · 2026 INSC 1092 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 340",
      "**Decided** 07 October 2026 · (Special Leave Petition (Civil) No. 30538 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Prashant Kumar Mishra* and Shree Chandrashekhar, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court upheld dismissal of an election petition presented by counsel. The petitioner said he had presented it himself, but the Court found that the evidence established at most that he was on the High Court premises. The Court read section 81 of the Representation of the People Act with Rule 7 of the Madras High Court Election Petitions Rules and the dismissal provision in section 86. (Paras. 1–8 and 18–24.)",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/v-pon-paneerselvam-versus-s-regupathy-and-others-1c9c0c18)",
      "## Which requirement was disputed",
      "The petitioner challenged an election through the statutory election-petition procedure. The Madras High Court dismissed the petition at the threshold because it had been presented by counsel. Rule 7 requires presentation by the petitioner in person before the specified officer. The special leave petition raised whether this was an essential feature of presentation or a procedural defect that could be overlooked. (Paras. 1–10.)",
      "## Why the precedents did not save this filing",
      "The Court distinguished a case involving a language requirement and explained the different treatment of sections 81 and 83. Section 86 expressly provides for dismissal for noncompliance with section 81. It also distinguished a decision finding substantial compliance when an advocate’s clerk presented the petition in the petitioner’s immediate presence. The petitioner did not argue that he had stood with counsel at the moment counsel presented the petition. The Court dismissed the special leave petition without adjudicating the election allegations. (Paras. 11–24.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
