    "title": "Why proof of grievous hurt did not establish robbery in Appa’s appeal",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Appa v. The State of Maharashtra · 2026 INSC 1091 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 331",
      "**Decided** 07 October 2026 · (Criminal Appeal No. 4766 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Prashant Kumar Mishra* and Shree Chandrashekhar, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court replaced a conviction for causing hurt during robbery with a conviction for voluntarily causing grievous hurt. It found sufficient evidence of the injury but insufficient evidence of robbery or an attempt to rob. The different ingredients changed the offence and the sentence. (Paras. 7–15.)",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/appa-versus-the-state-of-maharashtra-407207d6)",
      "## What the prosecution had to prove",
      "The trial court convicted under section 394 of the Indian Penal Code, and the High Court affirmed. That section required proof of robbery or attempted robbery as well as hurt. The complainant alleged that jewellery had been snatched. The Court examined the testimony and physical evidence. The jewellery was not recovered, and the surrounding evidence did not establish the robbery element beyond reasonable doubt. (Paras. 1–10.)",
      "## Why the Court still found grievous hurt",
      "The Court found that witness and medical evidence supported the loss of a tooth after the appellant struck the complainant. That injury fits the statutory definition of grievous hurt. The Court considered the separate evidence of a hand injury and the lack of a weapon linked to it. It partly allowed the appeal, substituted section 325, reduced the jail term to time already served and retained the ₹1,000 fine. Release was subject to custody in any other case. (Paras. 11–15.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
