    "citation": "2026 INSC 1090",
    "date": "2026-10-07",
    "title": "Why Mepco’s electricity subsidy was treated as taxable revenue",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** M/s Mepco Industries Ltd. v. Commissioner of Income Tax, Madurai · 2026 INSC 1090 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 318",
      "**Decided** 07 October 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 8694 of 2012)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Prashant Kumar Mishra* and Shree Chandrashekhar, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court held that the electricity subsidy Mepco received for assessment year 1997–98 was a revenue receipt. It looked to the purpose of the scheme and how the subsidy operated in practice. The payment reduced a running expense of a business already in production. (Paras. 1–2 and 19–24.)",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/m-s-mepco-industries-ltd-versus-commissioner-of-income-tax-madurai-e3e62b8c)",
      "## Why the purpose of the scheme mattered",
      "Mepco claimed that the subsidy of ₹16,20,745 was capital in character. The assessment and subsequent decisions treated it as taxable revenue. The Court applied the purpose test, which asks what the subsidy is designed to do. A scheme designed to help establish a capital-intensive undertaking can produce a different result from one designed to reduce operating expenses. (Paras. 2–20.)",
      "## How the Court applied that test",
      "The Court read the scheme’s operative terms alongside its general industrial-development objective. The concession was quantified by reference to electricity charges after the unit had been established and begun production. The possibility that lower costs might free funds for other investment did not establish a government contribution to the unit’s capital. The Court dismissed the appeal. (Paras. 19–24.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
