    "title": "Why a drink made from powder did not determine the tax category",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Addl. Commr. Commercial Tax & Ors. v. Cadila Health Care Ltd. & Anr. · 2026 INSC 1078 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 277",
      "**Decided** 05 October 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 9788 of 2013)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Manmohan* and Arun Palli, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court held that the tax classification turned on the goods as supplied at the taxable event. The fact that a consumer could make a beverage by mixing a preparation with liquid did not make the product sold a beverage under the entry at issue. (Paras. 25–32.)",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/addl-commr-commercial-tax-ors-versus-cadila-health-care-ltd-anr-5700ea6a)",
      "## The dispute over the products",
      "The tax authorities argued that Cadila’s GRD products came within the entry for non-alcoholic drinks and beverages. The dispute was whether to classify the preparations by the goods the buyer received or by a drink that could be made later. The Court examined the relevant Madhya Pradesh commercial and entry tax setting and how the products were supplied. (Paras. 1–18.)",
      "## Why the form at the time of sale mattered",
      "Tax attached to the transaction in the goods in the form sold. The Court treated consumer preparation as a later act. It distinguished the case of a liquid sharbat and read inclusive wording in context. It concluded the goods at issue were outside the beverage entry and dismissed the appeals. The holding depends on the words of the relevant entry and the characteristics at supply. (Paras. 19–32.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
