    "lead": "The Supreme Court allowed an appeal against the refusal to quash a complaint and charge sheet. It assessed the history between the parties and the rival complaints and concluded the High Court should have exercised its section 482 power in the circumstances shown by the record. (Paras. 9–15.)",
    "body": [
      "## The two accounts before the Court",
      "The complainant alleged sexual assault and related misconduct. The appellant disputed the account and said the case followed police complaints he had made. The Court examined the competing accounts and the documented sequence, including the earlier complaints and the criminal case that followed. The allegations should be read as the parties’ respective accounts. (Paras. 2–10.)",
      "## The decision and its scope",
      "The Court concluded that the parties had been in a consensual relationship that deteriorated and gave rise to mutual allegations. It set aside the High Court order, quashed the charge sheet and directed that the case should not continue against the appellant or the other accused in that FIR. It also directed that proceedings on the two earlier complaints identified in the judgment need not continue. It tied those consequential directions to the facts. The appeal was allowed. (Paras. 11–15.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
