    "date": "2026-10-01",
    "title": "Why incomplete circumstantial evidence led to acquittal",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Sikkim criminal appeal · 2026 INSC 1073",
      "**Decided** 01 October 2026",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Ujjal Bhuyan and Atul S. Chandurkar,* JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court set aside convictions under the Penal Code and the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act because the circumstances relied on did not establish guilt beyond reasonable doubt. It also explained the limited role of an accused’s answers under section 313 of the Code of Criminal Procedure. (Paras. 8–11.)",
    "body": [
      "## Which links in the prosecution case were disputed",
      "The prosecution relied on circumstantial evidence, including whether the appellant was last seen with the child and material said to have been discovered on disclosure. The Court assessed those strands independently and together. The witnesses were uncertain on the last-seen account, and the place connected to the discovery was ordinarily visible to others. The evidence linking the items to the alleged events did not close the gaps. (Paras. 1–10.)",
      "## Why the accused’s answers could not fill the gap",
      "A section 313 examination gives the accused a chance to explain circumstances put to them. The Court considered those answers in light of the established evidence. It found too little independent proof to treat the answers as an additional link sufficient to sustain the conviction. It allowed the appeal, acquitted the appellant and ordered release unless he was required in another case. (Paras. 9–11.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
