    "title": "How Article 14 applied to a coach’s request for release to a national team",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Indian Kayaking and Canoeing Association v. Union Territory of J&K and Ors. · 2026 INSC 981 · [2026] 9 S.C.R. 612",
      "**Decided** 10 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 12801 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Aravind Kumar and Vipul M. Pancholi, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court allowed the national kayaking and canoeing association’s appeal concerning refusal to release a government employee for a national coaching assignment. It assessed whether the decision took relevant facts into account, rather than assuming selection by the association automatically conferred a right to leave.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/indian-kayaking-and-canoeing-association-versus-union-territory-of-j-k-a-67a079f9)",
      "## Why the request was refused",
      "The association selected the coach for an Asian Games preparatory programme and asked the Union Territory to make her services available. After proceedings before a Single Judge, the Administration relied on a pending inquiry in refusing permission. The Division Bench upheld the decision based on the applicable service requirements and the lack of clearance.",
      "## What the Court required of the Administration",
      "The Supreme Court explained that Article 14 applies to discretionary decisions as well as statutory ones. Pending proceedings matter according to their circumstances, alongside the purpose and public significance of the assignment. The Court set aside the refusal to the extent it concerned the identified assignment and directed the authorities to issue the required permission and relieving documents by 15 September 2026. The permission covered the assignment and associated travel, with a duty to report back immediately afterwards. The Court left the merits and continuation of any departmental proceedings open. It also left intact the High Court’s request for an appropriate framework governing similar requests for public employees. No costs were ordered. (Judgment, analysis and paras. 39–41.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
