    "title": "What happens when a tribunal’s e-filing system prevents an appeal from being lodged?",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Regional Provident Fund Commissioner-II v. Ms. Mamta Binani & Ors. · 2026 INSC 1003 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 196",
      "**Decided** 10 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 12962 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Dipankar Datta* and Sheel Nagu, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court restored an insolvency appeal that arrived a day beyond the outer filing period because the tribunal’s e-filing system had failed. The tribunal must still determine whether the appellant established sufficient cause for filing beyond the initial 30 days.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/regional-provident-fund-commissioner-ii-versus-ms-mamta-binani-ors-0922a2cd)",
      "## What went wrong with filing",
      "The provident fund authority challenged approval of a resolution plan for Rolta India. It attempted to file within the period in which delay could be condoned, but a backend fault prevented the portal from accepting the appeal. The NCLAT treated the completed filing on 30 January 2026 as outside the statutory ceiling.",
      "## Why the Court restored the application",
      "The Court accepted that the tribunal lacked power simply to condone delay beyond the statutory maximum. It instead relied on the principle that a litigant should not suffer from the court’s own failure to accept a filing. It set aside the dismissal and directed reconsideration of sufficient cause under section 61(2). If the tribunal condones the delay within its lawful powers, it can then hear the appeal. Each party was to bear its own costs. (Paras. 23–25 and preceding reasoning.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
