    "title": "Why the Court intervened in S.S. Das’s compulsory retirement",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** S.S. Das v. Union of India · 2026 INSC 980 · [2026] 10 S.C.R. 1",
      "**Decided** 09 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 3215 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Sheel Nagu and Dipankar Datta,* JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court found that compulsory retirement under Fundamental Rule 56(j) was improperly exercised against an Indian Trade Service officer. It reviewed the basis of the decision and the manner in which the administration considered the officer’s record.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/s-s-das-versus-union-of-india-d2c5690c)",
      "## Why the record mattered",
      "Das had served for decades and had recently been regularly promoted to the Senior Administrative Grade. He was compulsorily retired in May 2018, several years before normal retirement. His representation and challenges before the tribunal and High Court did not undo the decision. The Supreme Court examined the service material and review process.",
      "## What the judgment means for this power",
      "Compulsory retirement in the public interest has a distinct function from punishment, but the decision still needs a rational foundation in the relevant record. Review can address arbitrariness or perversity rather than substituting a court’s own personnel assessment. The Court granted relief on the record before it and left the Union free to pursue recovery of the compensation and cost component from responsible officers according to law. Because Das had already reached retirement age, the Court granted the service benefits he would have received without the order, including notional promotion if a junior had been promoted in the intervening period. It directed the department to give him a formal farewell, awarded ₹9 lakh for loss of reputation and ₹6 lakh in costs, and ordered payment within three months. (Paras. 66–73.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
