    "title": "Who decides whether a railway contract claim falls outside arbitration?",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** M/s GVV Constructions Private Limited v. The Union of India & Ors. · 2026 INSC 976 · [2026] 9 S.C.R. 561",
      "**Decided** 08 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 12525 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Pamidighantam Sri Narasimha and Alok Aradhe,* JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court clarified that a High Court’s direction to apply the contract did not prevent the arbitral tribunal from deciding its own jurisdiction over a dispute arising from termination.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/m-s-gvv-constructions-private-limited-versus-the-union-of-india-ors-f621ba23)",
      "## How the issue reached the Court",
      "The contractor challenged termination through a writ petition. The Division Bench allowed it to raise claims in arbitration and said the tribunal could decide them according to the contract. The tribunal framed issues that included validity of termination and whether some claims were excepted matters or otherwise outside arbitration. The contractor was concerned that the High Court’s wording had already limited those questions.",
      "## What the clarification preserves",
      "The Court read the railway contract’s excepted-matters and arbitration clauses together. Excluding a matter from arbitration does not by itself eliminate judicial scrutiny of the underlying decision. The tribunal’s power under section 16 to determine the scope of arbitration was preserved. The Court clarified the High Court’s direction and disposed of the appeal without costs. (Judgment, analysis and paras. 22–24.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
