    "citation": "2026 INSC 956",
    "date": "2026-09-07",
    "title": "Why the Court restored clearances for a biomedical waste treatment facility",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** M/s Punahchakran Private Limited v. Indotech Waste Solution & Ors. · 2026 INSC 956 · [2026] 9 S.C.R. 457",
      "**Decided** 07 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 7367 of 2024)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [J.B. Pardiwala* and Manoj Misra, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court set aside the National Green Tribunal’s cancellation of Punahchakran’s second environmental clearance and consent to establish. The judgment distinguishes the stages of environmental appraisal, land arrangements and pollution-control consent.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/m-s-punahchakran-private-limited-versus-indotech-waste-solution-ors-e94c413b)",
      "## How the approvals were challenged",
      "The dispute concerned a common biomedical waste treatment facility in Uttar Pradesh. The Tribunal objected to the land arrangements and the assessment process, including use of the first terms of reference. The Court examined the 2006 environmental impact notification alongside the 2016 facility guidelines and considered whether the pollution control board could grant a relaxation in minimum land size.",
      "## Why timing and the project description mattered",
      "The Court held that the 2016 guidelines were mandatory even though they had not been published in the Official Gazette. It found that the one-acre rule could be relaxed in the settings listed by the guidelines if the Central Pollution Control Board was consulted and additional controls imposed. This site was rural, and those additional steps had been taken. (Para. 110(i)–(iii).)",
      "At the Form 1 stage, identifying a prospective site was sufficient. At environmental appraisal, the Court distinguished site identification from the credible evidence of intended acquisition required under the applicable guidance. It also distinguished consent to establish from consent to operate. The pollution authority could issue consent to establish before clearance after ascertaining whether the clearance regime applied. Prior environmental clearance was required before consent to operate, and any necessary land-size relaxation had to precede consent to establish. In this case, both the land allotment and the relaxation predated the second approvals. (Para. 110(iv)–(viii).)",
      "The Court also examined whether the additional plot that prompted the second terms of reference remained part of the proposal. Once the proposal reverted to its earlier footing and the second terms were withdrawn, the Court found no bar to using the first terms on the facts. It restored the position by setting aside the Tribunal order and directed circulation to the Tribunal’s benches. The judgment addresses the approvals actually challenged and their regulatory context. (Paras. 109–114 and preceding analysis.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
