    "title": "Can the teacher education regulator require annual performance reports?",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** The National Council for Teachers Education v. Association of NCTE Approved Colleges Trust and Ors. · 2026 INSC 953 · [2026] 9 S.C.R. 441",
      "**Decided** 03 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 12179 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Pamidighantam Sri Narasimha and Alok Aradhe, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court upheld the power to require recognised teacher education institutions to file annual performance appraisal reports. It examined both the Council’s statutory functions and the Executive Committee’s role in carrying them out.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/the-national-council-for-teachers-education-versus-association-of-ncte-a-b3bd1c46)",
      "## Why institutions challenged the notice",
      "A public notice required online reporting and a payment. The institutions argued that only the Council could require reports and that the Executive Committee lacked the necessary authority. A single judge dismissed the challenge, but the Delhi High Court’s Division Bench reversed that decision.",
      "## What the statute allowed",
      "Section 12(k) of the National Council for Teacher Education Act allows the Council to develop appraisal and accountability arrangements. The Court found that the Council had considered the issue and authorised implementation of a formal reporting mechanism, so the Executive Committee was carrying out the Council’s decision. It also treated the process fee as incidental to the regulatory function. It allowed the appeal, set aside the Division Bench judgment and ordered no costs. (Judgment, statutory analysis and paras. 24–26.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
