    "title": "Why the Supreme Court set aside a customs penalty that relied on false citations",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Vijay Ghanshyam Gadiya v. Union of India & Anr. · 2026 INSC 947 · [2026] 9 S.C.R. 423",
      "**Decided** 02 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 12155 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Dipankar Datta* and Sheel Nagu, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court set aside a customs penalty and sent the matter for a fresh decision after checking authorities relied on in the adjudication. It found that some authorities did not exist or had false citations, while some real cases did not support the propositions attributed to them.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/vijay-ghanshyam-gadiya-versus-union-of-india-anr-9f7d27b5)",
      "## What had gone wrong in the decision",
      "A customs adjudicating officer imposed a ₹425 crore penalty for alleged misdeclaration of a consignment. The High Court declined to interfere. In the Supreme Court the appellant challenged the reliability of authorities used by the officer. The Court’s verification showed problems at both the citation and proposition levels.",
      "## What the Court said about AI and what happens next",
      "The Court acknowledged that a tool can assist with the adjudicatory process, but stressed that responsibility for evaluating evidence and checking the law remains with the decision-maker. It treated reliance on dubious authorities as fatal to the order. The underlying customs proceedings were revived for a fresh decision by a different officer of the same rank. Any action concerning the original officer was left to the appointing authority. The remand leaves the merits open. (Judgment, paras. 7–11 and preceding verification.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
