    "lead": "The Supreme Court extended the benefit of doubt to the appellant and set aside his convictions. It found the prosecution had failed to prove the links connecting him with the child or with the place of the offence.",
    "body": [
      "## The evidence the prosecution relied on",
      "The case depended on circumstances including a last-seen theory and an alleged extra-judicial confession. The trial court convicted and the High Court affirmed. The Supreme Court examined whether the chronology, witness accounts and other evidence reliably placed the appellant with the child at the relevant time.",
      "## Why the links did not establish guilt",
      "The Court found major gaps on association, time and place. It also considered testimony that the police had brought the appellant in before the alleged confession, which reinforced doubts about that part of the prosecution case. The Court found that the total evidence fell short of the criminal standard. It recorded that it had directed release when reserving judgment, subject to detention in another case, and allowed the appeal. (Paras. 46–50.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
