    "title": "Why Karnataka’s land-record authority could not reopen this order after three years",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** M.R.R. Setty (Dead), by Lrs. v. Government of Karnataka and Others · 2026 INSC 944 · [2026] 9 S.C.R. 391",
      "**Decided** 02 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No(s). 12163 and 12164 of 2026)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [Sanjay Kumar* and Sanjeev Sachdeva, JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court quashed a 2014 land-record notice insofar as it concerned the appellants’ property. The statutory time limit barred the attempted reopening.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/m-r-r-setty-dead-by-lrs-versus-government-of-karnataka-and-others-632e321d)",
      "## What triggered the notice",
      "A landowner had claimed title through historic sale deeds and developed the land after obtaining municipal approvals. A third party alleged encroachment on Yediyur Lake. The Joint Director or Registrar of Land Records cancelled an earlier enquiry order and proposed to examine the land again. The owners challenged the notice as outside the time permitted under section 56 of the Karnataka Land Revenue Act.",
      "## Why the deadline came first",
      "The Court read the proviso to section 56(3) as barring the exercise of revision after three years in relation to the unappealed order at issue. That went to the power to start the proceeding. The Court set aside the Division Bench judgment and review order to the relevant extent, quashed the notice as to the appellants’ land and left the parties to bear their own costs. The ruling resolves this use of revision rather than the underlying allegation on its merits. (Paras. 16–18 and preceding analysis.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
