    "citation": "2026 INSC 941",
    "date": "2026-09-02",
    "title": "Who had to track individual client collateral when a trading member defaulted?",
    "metadata": [
      "**Case** Edelweiss Custodial Services Limited v. NSE Clearing Ltd. & Anr. · 2026 INSC 941 · [2026] 9 S.C.R. 332",
      "**Decided** 02 September 2026 · (Civil Appeal No. 31 of 2024)",
      "**Bench as recorded by SCR** [J.B. Pardiwala and K. Vinod Chandran,* JJ.]"
    ],
    "lead": "The Supreme Court set aside orders against professional clearing members after examining the rules and systems in force when trading members defaulted. The dispute concerned sales of collateral provided through trading members, including collateral from clients whose own positions were not in debit.",
    "body": [
      "[Read the judgment and download the SCR PDF](https://thelegalchronicles.com/judgments/supreme-court/2026/edelweiss-custodial-services-limited-versus-nse-clearing-ltd-anr-e600f938)",
      "## How the collateral moved",
      "Futures and options clients deposited security with trading members, who passed collateral to professional clearing members. Following default by the trading members, collateral was sold. Investors challenged the liquidation and the clearing members argued that the applicable arrangements did not give them client-level balances or impose a duty to verify them before each sale.",
      "## Why the applicable framework mattered",
      "The Court considered the obligations at each level of the market structure and the information available at the time of the sales. It held that the framework did not impose a statutory duty on clearing members to verify individual clients’ positions before liquidation and did not give them visibility of those positions. It found no illegality in the liquidation on the record before it and rejected the basis for compelling restitution from the clearing members. Four appeals were allowed, the challenged committee and Tribunal orders were set aside, and the remaining appeal seeking the benefit of those orders was rejected. The decision must be read against the rules and reporting mechanisms applicable when these sales took place. (Judgment, analysis and paras. 46–58.)",
      "*AI-assisted editorial explanation. Independent human legal review has not been performed.*"
    ],
