      "## What compliance reports covered",
      "The Court considered progress on toilets and washing facilities, sanitary products, menstrual hygiene corners, disposal systems, teacher sensitisation, district notices and inspections. It recorded progress while finding that implementation still needed work. It also considered the practical effects of poor waste segregation and limited processing infrastructure.",
      "## What the Court said about product choices",
      "The order encouraged a gradual move toward environmentally preferable and scientifically validated options, accounting for hygiene, infrastructure and awareness. It discussed biodegradable pads and reusable alternatives and stressed the educational work needed to support their use. The Court also directed district education officers to form teams for surprise school inspections. It required the next reports to describe remaining gaps, their causes and the time needed to close them, including where schools lacked accessible, functional, gender-segregated toilets with usable water. (Paras. 18–23.)",
      "The Court disposed of the interim application and another writ petition in the stated terms and listed the main matter again. The record of progress belongs to this order’s date. Later compliance requires separate verification. (Judgment, compliance discussion and paras. 24–26.)",
