      "## Why the statement mattered",
      "The investment authority alleged that later corporate transactions violated a statement recorded before the Commercial Court. IQuest had said in a counter-affidavit that it had decided at the time not to proceed with a proposed acquisition. The High Court dismissed contempt, treating the statement as clarification rather than a solemn promise.",
      "## What remained for the execution courts",
      "The Supreme Court agreed that the statement did not meet the threshold for a binding undertaking. Questions about whether particular companies or assets were within reach of the decree belonged in execution on the relevant evidence. The Court also directed the judgment debtor and the respondent entities, jointly and severally, to furnish ₹200 crore in additional security with the Supreme Court Registry within two weeks. Encashment was subject to the outcome of execution. (Paras. 69–75.) It left questions about reaching the assets of related entities for the Commercial Courts. It preserved the parties’ contentions, disposed of the NCLAT appeals in the terms stated, and directed the Hyderabad and Ranga Reddy Commercial Courts to decide the execution petitions and applications within four months. Contempt and execution answered different questions. (Judgment, undertaking analysis and final directions.)",
