      "## What the family challenged",
      "A tribunal awarded compensation after a 48-year-old private security worker died in a motor accident. The High Court increased the award to ₹11,00,672. The family claimed that he earned ₹9,000 a month. The Supreme Court’s calculation kept the High Court’s award as its starting point. The increase reflects the Court’s treatment of consortium, the compensation for loss of companionship and care. (Paras. 3.1 and 5–8.)",
      "## How the Court calculated the increase",
      "Applying the consortium authorities it cited, the Court awarded ₹48,400 each under that head to the spouse and three dependent children. The resulting increase over the High Court’s award was ₹1,46,600. It directed payment of that increase with 7.5% interest from filing of the petition until realisation, deposit within six weeks and equal distribution after verification. The children’s status as legal representatives and dependants was not disputed in this case. (Paras. 6–11.)",
